The ferx package's own MIT license does not change the terms of the code in
this section: anyone distributing the compiled package distributes that code
too, and must follow its licenses.


3. Using a modified ferx-search
-------------------------------

ferx-search is linked statically. The LGPL entitles you to rebuild the package
against a modified ferx-search, and the package's build supports that directly:

  1. Get the source of this ferx version: the source package (ferx_<version>.tar.gz)
     or the matching tag of https://github.com/FeRx-NLME/ferx-r. Unpack it so
     the package directory is, say, work/ferx.

  2. Clone ferx-core next to it, as work/ferx-core, at the commit named in
     THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt (also in work/ferx/src/rust/Cargo.lock):

       git clone https://github.com/FeRx-NLME/ferx-core work/ferx-core
       git -C work/ferx-core checkout <commit>

  3. Edit work/ferx-core/crates/ferx-search.

  4. Install: R CMD INSTALL work/ferx

src/Makevars finds the sibling ../ferx-core checkout and builds ferx-core,
ferx-tools and ferx-search from it instead of the pinned revision; its last
line reads "ferx: the sibling supplied ALL of ...". The build needs a stable
Rust toolchain and cargo, as any source install of ferx does.


